<div>
<div class="n-label">1875, and a diagonal</div>
<h2 class="n-h2" id="why-h" style="margin-top:12px;max-width:20ch;color:var(--n-evergreen)">Why the street runs at an angle</h2>
</div>
<div class="n-read">
<p class="n-body" style="margin:0">A rail line built through the Left Hand valley in the 1870s set where the town site would be. The plat was recorded in 1875 by Porter T. Hinman and Ambrose S. Murray, laid out on a diagonal grid straddling the tracks &#8212; which is why Second Avenue and the streets around it sit at an angle to the county road pattern that arrived later.</p>
<p class="n-body" style="margin:18px 0 0">Brick and false-front commercial buildings went up along the avenue to serve the surrounding farms and the trains that carried their crops. Through the twentieth century the houses spread outward and Cottonwood Square added a second cluster of shops, but the historic block was kept rather than replaced. That decision is the whole reason this page exists.</p>
<p class="n-small" style="margin:18px 0 0">Dates and names from the Niwot Historical Society&#8217;s history timeline, which the full history page cites entry by entry.</p>
<div class="n-btns" style="margin-top:22px">
<a class="n-btn n-btn--ghost" href="/history/">The history of Niwot</a>
<a class="n-btn n-btn--ghost" href="https://niwothistoricalsociety.org/" rel="noopener">Historical Society <span aria-hidden="true">&#8599;</span></a>
</div>
